ow": 8}`.\n\nI must block White’s immediate threat, so I will play E8, and then evaluate the impact of the move. I will play E8.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: White has a dangerous configuration with a diagonal line of three stones at H5, G6, and F7. This is an "Open 3" because both ends (E8 and I4) are empty and unblocked. If White plays at E8, they will form an "Open 4" (connecting H5-G6-F7-E8), which has two open ends (D9 and I4). An Open 4 is a guaranteed win because Black can only block one side, allowing White to make 5 on the next turn. Therefore, Black\'s immediate priority must be to block this threat. E8 is the best blocking position as it is more central and has better potential for future connections with Black\'s stones at E6 and G10 compared to the alternative blocking spot at I4.\n\nReason: White has a lethal threat of forming an "Open 4" on the diagonal H5-G6-F7 by playing at E8. Black must block at E8 to prevent this immediate loss. E8 is preferred over I4 as it disrupts White\'s shape in the center and offers better follow-up potential for Black.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "E", "row": 8}'}
