mething else, but it\'s not. I\'ve got to play J11 to prevent White from getting five in a row.\n\nOkay, I\'ve confirmed that the only viable move is to play J11. This blocks White\'s potential winning play, and while it might not be a winning move for Black, it\'s the only move that prevents an immediate loss. The reasoning is: White has a diagonal chain of four stones that\'s one move away from a win. I have no winning moves, so I *must* block to survive.\nSo my move is J11.\n{"col": "J", "row": 11}\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board state shows a critical threat from White. White has established a diagonal line of four stones located at F7, G8, H9, and I10. The top-left end of this line (at E6) is blocked by a Black stone, but the bottom-right end (at J11) is currently empty. This formation constitutes a "Four" (specifically a bounded four), meaning White can play at J11 on their next turn to form a line of five stones and win the game. Black does not have any immediate winning moves (such as a pre-existing four-in-a-row) to ignore this threat.\n\nReason: Because White has a 4-in-a-row threat that will result in an immediate win if not addressed, Black\'s only viable option is to block the line. The open spot to block is J11. Failing to play here will result in a loss on the next turn.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "J", "row": 11}'}
