ne has an open end at C11. If White plays at C11, they achieve five in a row and win. I check the other direction for the other end of this "4" (top-right side) is H6 (Black). Thus it is a sleeping 4.\n\nI then search for any winning moves for Black, any openings to create an immediate "five" or a "four" which would force a win. There does not appear to be any way to construct a win immediately. There\'s not a lot of immediate opportunities to create a win or block white.  It is important that I check and find out if White has 4 (D10, E9, F8, G7) which when completed to 5 creates a win. I check if Black can create 4-in-a-row to threaten White. I analyze any "Open 4" threats for Black, but conclude that any attempt for Black to create a 4-in-a-row will result in a win by White on the next move. Any strategy is irrelevant here - Black MUST stop White at C11. I must block White\'s diagonal line at C11 (bottom left to top right).\n\nSince Black has no immediate winning moves, I recognize that blocking White\'s win is the top priority.\n\nI also identify the only winning move: Black *must* block at C11. If I do not play at C11, White instantly wins on the next move. This is the only move. I have made a decision. Black needs to play C11 to defend against the threat.\n\nThere appears to be no other threat that could overrule the move at C11.\n\nAfter a final verification to confirm the coordinates, the move is clear: {"col": "C", "row": 11}. I can defend.\n\nThis move is the only one to be able to be played.\n\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation:\nThe current board situation presents a critical defensive moment for Black.\n1.  **Analyze White\'s Threats:**\n    *   **Diagonal (D10-G7):** White has four stones in a row diagonally at D10, E9, F8, and G7. The top-right end at H6 is blocked by a Black stone. However, the bottom-left end at **C11** is empty. If White plays C11, they will have 5 connected stones and win immediately. This is a "Sleep 4" becoming a "5".\n    *   **Row 7:*