r with my line?" No. There are no other "Four" threats. I can\'t win. I must block.\n\nI look at **J3**.  I confirm the coordinates (J is column 10, 3 is row 3).\n\n**4. The Winning\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: The current board shows a critical situation. White has successfully built a diagonal line of four stones consisting of I4, H5, G6, and F7. This line is blocked at one end by Black\'s stone at E8, but the other end at J3 is open. This constitutes an immediate lethal threat ("Four"), as White can play at J3 on the next turn to complete a 5-in-a-row and win the game. While Black has a very promising offensive setup with the stones G5, H6, and J8 (which could form an Open 4 if Black played I7), Black cannot ignore White\'s immediate winning threat.\n\nReason: White has a "Four" (4 connected stones) on the diagonal from I4 to F7. The spot J3 is the only open end to this line (E8 is blocked). If Black does not play at J3, White will play there next to form 5-in-a-row. Therefore, Black is forced to play J3 to block the win.\n\nAnswer: {"col": "J", "row": 3}'}
